[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

How would you like to approach this? Feel free to take a moment to gather your thoughts first.

[candidate · 0:07] Let me take a second to structure this.

So the key tension is revenue is up 15% but margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's a huge drop, roughly a quarter of what it was. Since revenue grew, the story is almost certainly on the cost side, though I don't want to rule out revenue mix yet.

I'd break it into three buckets:

First, revenue quality — is the 15% growth coming from new stores, more transactions per store, or price increases? Growth from opening new locations could be dragging margin if new stores aren't mature yet.

Second, costs, split into COGS (coffee beans, milk, cups, food) and operating costs (labor, rent, utilities, marketing, corporate overhead). I'd want to see which line items grew faster than revenue.

Third, external factors — commodity prices, wage inflation, competition.

To start, do we have a cost breakdown as a percentage of revenue for both years? That would let me isolate where the 18 points went.
