[interviewer · CLARIFY · 0:09] Okay. I'll come to that data shortly. Before I give you data, is that structure MECE — what's missing or overlapping?

[candidate · 0:15] Fair challenge. A couple of issues.

Overlap: my "external factors" bucket isn't really separate — commodity prices show up inside COGS, wage inflation inside labor. It's a driver of the other two, not a parallel branch. I'd fold it in as a "why" layer rather than a third bucket.

Gap: I framed it as revenue and costs, but profit margin is profit over revenue, so I should be explicit that revenue itself has two levers — price per ticket and volume — and that mix matters. A shift toward lower-margin items, say more food or more cold drinks with higher input costs, could hurt margin even with revenue growing.

Also missing: anything below the operating line. Net margin, not gross, so interest expense, depreciation from a build-out, or taxes could be part of it. If they've been financing 200 locations with debt, that's real.

So cleaner version: margin change equals revenue-side effects (price, volume, mix) plus cost-side effects (COGS, operating, below-the-line), with external drivers explaining each.
