[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:12] Okay. Going back to your bean calculation: you derived the bean share as a residual from COGS. Walk me through whether that residual holds up given the 40% bean cost increase I gave you.

[candidate · 1:21] Let me pressure-test it, because I think I made an error a moment ago.

If bean prices are up 40% per pound and bean volume grows with revenue at 15%, bean dollars are up 1.40 × 1.15 = 1.61, so 61%. As a share of revenue that's 1.61/1.15 = 1.40 — the volume cancels and you just get the price effect. So beans go from 10.5 points to 10.5 × 1.40 = 14.7 points, not the 12.8 I said. I double-counted the revenue growth before.

So the gap is 20.3 versus 14.7, about 5.6 points unexplained, not 7.5. Still large, but smaller.

The deeper issue: the residual is only as good as the assumption that COGS equals beans plus those three categories. If there's anything else in COGS — syrups, merchandise, new food lines, or distribution — the residual dumps all of it onto beans and overstates the anomaly. Do we know those four categories are exhaustive?
