[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:32] I don't have a further breakdown of COGS beyond those categories, so treat that as an open caveat. Beyond what we've discussed, what else could the client do to reverse the margin decline?

[candidate · 1:39] Fair. Let me lay out the levers beyond fixing bean usage.

Pricing is the most obvious untouched one. Prices haven't moved in two years while input costs rose sharply — that's the single biggest self-inflicted piece. Specialty coffee tends to be relatively price-inelastic and competitors are facing the same commodity environment, so a 5-8% increase likely sticks. Rough math: on a 6% margin, even 5 points of price flowing mostly to the bottom line roughly doubles profitability. I'd also reprice selectively — raise on the bean-heavy drinks that are driving the usage problem rather than across the board.

Procurement: they're clearly exposed to spot prices. Forward contracts or hedging won't recover past losses but stops the bleeding, and at 200 locations there's scale to negotiate.

Mix and menu engineering: push higher-margin items — food, merchandise, cold brew — and consider cup-size or recipe standardization.

Overhead is up 2 points; smaller, but worth asking whether corporate costs scaled with new store openings.

What I'd avoid is cutting labor — it's flat at 22% and cutting it risks service quality.
